WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.852 --> 00:00:13.036
It was the beauty of the world that inspired Pierre-Auguste Renoir.

2
00:00:14.297 --> 00:00:20.379
He loved to paint families strolling in the park, young couples dancing at a ball, and children playing

3
00:00:20.419 --> 00:00:20.940
in the sun.

4
00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:28.403
He began as a humble painter decorating porcelain vases, and eventually he became one of the world's most

5
00:00:28.463 --> 00:00:31.544
significant painters and the father of Impressionism.

6
00:00:32.636 --> 00:00:37.139
Renoir was born in 1841 in Limoges, a small French town.

7
00:00:38.279 --> 00:00:41.221
His father was a tailor and his mother a dressmaker.

8
00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:46.564
When Renoir was four years old, his family moved to Paris in search of a better life.

9
00:00:47.264 --> 00:00:52.547
The family lived very close to the Louvre, a large museum with the richest collection of art in

10
00:00:52.647 --> 00:01:00.672
France. The young Renoir spent long days there, looking at the old masters' paintings, deeply inspired by their

11
00:01:00.692 --> 00:01:07.328
beauty. His favorite artist was Peter Paul Rubens, whose art continued to influence him throughout his life.

12
00:01:09.109 --> 00:01:12.772
As a young schoolboy, Renoir covered his schoolbooks with drawings.

13
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:18.376
His parents urged him to put his artistic talents to use and helped him get a job at

14
00:01:18.396 --> 00:01:19.477
a porcelain factory.

15
00:01:20.757 --> 00:01:25.060
There Renoir learned to paint delicate flower patterns on porcelain vases.

16
00:01:25.981 --> 00:01:29.684
He was a fast learner and soon became a very successful painter.

17
00:01:30.932 --> 00:01:36.716
His career came to an end, however, when a new mechanical process for printing designs onto chinaware was

18
00:01:36.776 --> 00:01:43.220
introduced. To earn a living, he copied onto women's fans the paintings he had seen at the Louvre

19
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:43.941
as a child.

20
00:01:44.841 --> 00:01:46.602
Then he sold the fans on the street.

21
00:01:47.763 --> 00:01:52.306
But he knew that he wanted to do more with his life he dreamed of becoming an artist

22
00:01:52.666 --> 00:01:55.388
and creating paintings just like the ones he was copying.

23
00:01:56.069 --> 00:02:00.672
In 1861 Renoir was penniless but determined to become an artist.

24
00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.764
He enrolled in an art class in the studio of Charles Gleyre, where he met other struggling artists

25
00:02:06.784 --> 00:02:08.425
who would become his lifelong friends.

26
00:02:09.846 --> 00:02:16.370
Together with Claude Monet, Alfred Sisley, and Camille Pissaro he would paint all day, and then in the

27
00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.532
evenings, they would gather at cafes to discuss art.

28
00:02:20.813 --> 00:02:26.397
One of their favorite gathering places was the Inn of Mother Anthony, which Renoir immortalized in one of

29
00:02:26.437 --> 00:02:27.077
his paintings.

30
00:02:28.238 --> 00:02:33.927
The young artists were searching for new ways of painting and soon became known as the Impressionists.

31
00:02:35.508 --> 00:02:37.931
The Impressionists believed in a new way of seeing.

32
00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:43.236
They used many brushstrokes of different colors right next to each other on the canvas.

33
00:02:44.357 --> 00:02:48.461
By this method, they captured a feeling of movement and light in their paintings.

34
00:02:49.682 --> 00:02:54.807
Instead of smoothing the surface of the painting like artists before them, the Impressionists would leave traces of

35
00:02:54.827 --> 00:02:56.008
their brushstrokes visible.

36
00:02:57.972 --> 00:03:01.053
They also went outside to paint, instead of staying in their studios.

37
00:03:02.773 --> 00:03:09.575
The Impressionists packed their paintbrushes, paints, and easels and set out into nature, searching for inspiring landscapes.

38
00:03:11.036 --> 00:03:13.997
This period of his studies was a difficult one for Renoir.

39
00:03:15.257 --> 00:03:20.638
With almost no money, he often thought about giving up, but his passion for painting kept him going.

40
00:03:21.979 --> 00:03:25.580
He kept thinking of the artists from the Louvre who he admired as a young child.

41
00:03:27.144 --> 00:03:29.566
His artist friends always gave him a helping hand.

42
00:03:30.847 --> 00:03:36.472
His friend Bazille offered to share his studio, and his friend Monet often gave him encouraging advice.

43
00:03:38.094 --> 00:03:42.678
By 1863 Renoir was still trying to find his own painting style.

44
00:03:43.638 --> 00:03:47.001
He was using dark and gloomy tones that never seemed right to him.

45
00:03:48.102 --> 00:03:52.326
One day he was out painting in a park when an old man came to see his work.

46
00:03:53.427 --> 00:03:57.641
He said to Renoir, "Your painting is good but why do you use such dark colors?

47
00:03:58.722 --> 00:04:00.222
Don't you see the beauty around you?"

48
00:04:02.123 --> 00:04:05.484
Renoir realized that he could paint the world as he wished to see it.

49
00:04:06.885 --> 00:04:09.186
He began using fresh and light colors.

50
00:04:10.186 --> 00:04:14.768
The old man turned out to be an established painter from Paris named Narcisse Diaz.

51
00:04:15.789 --> 00:04:20.330
He helped Renoir by buying him painting supplies and introducing him to art dealers.

52
00:04:21.131 --> 00:04:26.186
Renoir was able to get a few commissions to paint portraits, and this eased his financial worries.

53
00:04:27.967 --> 00:04:31.210
One of Renoir's favorite subjects was the life of Parisians.

54
00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:37.455
He loved to paint them dancing, walking in the park, enjoying a meal, or sitting at the opera.

55
00:04:38.095 --> 00:04:41.258
He wanted to express the joy he saw in everyday life.

56
00:04:42.238 --> 00:04:44.700
Renoir also loved to paint people socializing.

57
00:04:45.861 --> 00:04:51.246
His studio was very close to a popular dance hall in Paris, where he spent almost one year

58
00:04:51.366 --> 00:04:53.768
sketching and painting scenes of dancing couples.

59
00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.843
He was fascinated with the graceful movements of women when they danced.

60
00:04:59.505 --> 00:05:03.888
By the time Renoir reached his forties, he was a well-established painter in Paris.

61
00:05:04.709 --> 00:05:11.575
He lived well off his portrait commissions, often painting aristocrats, art dealers, and rich patrons.

62
00:05:11.635 --> 00:05:14.978
In 1883 he had his first solo exhibition in Paris.

63
00:05:16.159 --> 00:05:21.044
Even though he was becoming successful, he never stopped developing his own artistic style.

64
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:29.043
He traveled to Algeria in search of new and interesting subjects and painted the exotic landscapes he found

65
00:05:29.083 --> 00:05:36.186
there. At the end of his career, Renoir returned to working in a classical style, similar to the

66
00:05:36.226 --> 00:05:38.547
paintings he copied from the Louvre in his youth.

67
00:05:39.667 --> 00:05:46.170
Even though Renoir was crippled by arthritis and almost blind, he kept painting until his death in 1919.

68
00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:52.618
Through his art he created his own world full of light, happiness, and promise.

69
00:05:53.779 --> 00:05:58.084
His paintings continue to bring joy and optimism to millions of people today.
